1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,678
In the Tsinghua interview, you may well be asked:

2
00:00:02,678 --> 00:00:04,365
what's your favorite theorem?

3
00:00:04,433 --> 00:00:05,837
It sounds like small talk,

4
00:00:05,837 --> 00:00:08,656
but it's really the start of a chain of follow-up questions.

5
00:00:08,806 --> 00:00:11,441
Can you state it precisely? Can you prove it?

6
00:00:11,441 --> 00:00:14,401
Are the conditions necessary? Does it generalize?

7
00:00:14,401 --> 00:00:16,158
What does it mean to you?

8
00:00:16,233 --> 00:00:20,441
In this video, I want to share a theorem that I genuinely love,

9
00:00:20,441 --> 00:00:24,130
and one that holds up under exactly this kind of questioning.

10
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:26,346
Let's start with a small experiment.

11
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:28,649
Write down the primes, and try to split

12
00:00:28,649 --> 00:00:31,338
each one into a sum of two squares.

13
00:00:31,488 --> 00:00:37,227
Five is one plus four. Thirteen is four plus nine.

14
00:00:37,227 --> 00:00:43,210
Seventeen is one plus sixteen. Twenty-nine is four plus twenty-five.

15
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,117
But three, seven, eleven, nineteen, twenty-three:

16
00:00:47,117 --> 00:00:49,445
no matter how hard you try, they refuse.

17
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:53,105
The pattern hides in the remainder mod four.

18
00:00:53,255 --> 00:00:57,525
The odd primes that work all leave remainder one when divided by four.

19
00:00:57,525 --> 00:01:00,443
The ones that fail all leave remainder three.

20
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:05,222
How can a prime 'know', just from its remainder mod four,

21
00:01:05,222 --> 00:01:07,792
whether it is a sum of two squares?

22
00:01:07,942 --> 00:01:10,765
This is Fermat's two-square theorem.

23
00:01:10,915 --> 00:01:14,401
Today we'll answer the question with a proof that is only

24
00:01:14,401 --> 00:01:18,699
one sentence long, and with the windmill hiding behind it.

25
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:25,668
On Christmas Day, 1640, Fermat announced

26
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:28,465
this result in a letter to Marin Mersenne.

27
00:01:28,615 --> 00:01:32,995
That's why it's sometimes called Fermat's Christmas theorem.

28
00:01:33,145 --> 00:01:37,390
As usual, Fermat claimed he had a proof, but never wrote it down.

29
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:40,364
The first published proof came from Euler.

30
00:01:40,514 --> 00:01:44,171
It took him several years, until around 1749,

31
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:49,448
and he used Fermat's own favorite tool: the method of infinite descent.

32
00:01:49,533 --> 00:01:53,242
Later, Gauss placed the theorem inside a much bigger picture:

33
00:01:53,242 --> 00:01:54,818
the Gaussian integers.

34
00:01:54,968 --> 00:02:00,333
Minkowski gave another proof using the geometry of lattices.

35
00:02:00,483 --> 00:02:03,823
Each new proof revealed a new side of the same fact.

36
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:08,168
Then in 1990, Don Zagier condensed an earlier argument

37
00:02:08,168 --> 00:02:10,648
of Roger Heath-Brown into a single sentence,

38
00:02:10,648 --> 00:02:13,644
and published it in the American Mathematical Monthly.

39
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:16,010
That sentence looks like a magic trick.

40
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:17,708
You can check that it's correct,

41
00:02:17,708 --> 00:02:20,607
but you have no idea how anyone thought of it.

42
00:02:20,757 --> 00:02:23,760
Our job today is to take the trick apart.

43
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:29,549
First, let's state it precisely.

44
00:02:29,699 --> 00:02:34,932
Let p be an odd prime. Then p can be written as a sum of two

45
00:02:34,932 --> 00:02:40,740
integer squares if and only if p is congruent to one mod four.

46
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:47,456
Two small remarks. The prime two also works, since two is one plus one.

47
00:02:47,606 --> 00:02:52,716
And the representation is essentially unique, up to order and signs.

48
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:57,080
'If and only if' means two directions.

49
00:02:57,230 --> 00:03:01,798
Going from 'sum of two squares' to 'one mod

50
00:03:01,798 --> 00:03:04,910
four' is the necessary condition, and it's easy.

51
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:10,798
Going the other way, from 'one mod four' to 'a and b must exist',

52
00:03:10,798 --> 00:03:14,303
is the sufficient condition. That's the real challenge.

53
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,710
Let's take care of the easy direction first.

54
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:26,826
Any square leaves remainder zero or one when divided by four.

55
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:34,221
If n is even, n squared is a multiple of four. If n is odd, n squared,

56
00:03:34,221 --> 00:03:38,963
like nine, twenty-five or forty-nine, is one more than a multiple of four.

57
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:44,036
So a sum of two squares can only leave remainder zero,

58
00:03:44,036 --> 00:03:47,396
one, or two. Never three.

59
00:03:47,467 --> 00:03:52,173
An odd prime leaves remainder one or three.

60
00:03:52,323 --> 00:03:55,858
The ones with remainder three are ruled out from the start.

61
00:03:56,008 --> 00:04:01,228
That's the necessary direction, and all it needs is arithmetic mod four.

62
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:03,548
The real question is the converse:

63
00:04:03,548 --> 00:04:08,619
why must every prime that is one mod four actually split?

64
00:04:08,769 --> 00:04:12,773
For that, we need a completely different idea.

65
00:04:16,267 --> 00:04:19,944
Constructing a and b directly is hard,

66
00:04:19,944 --> 00:04:22,535
because we have no idea what they look like.

67
00:04:22,685 --> 00:04:25,243
Zagier's idea is: don't search, count.

68
00:04:25,243 --> 00:04:30,575
And not even count exactly, just count mod two.

69
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:36,341
Imagine a dance. Everyone either pairs up with a partner, or stands alone.

70
00:04:36,491 --> 00:04:41,324
If the total number of people is odd, someone must be standing alone.

71
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:45,208
In mathematics, this pairing is called an involution:

72
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:49,616
a map f which, applied twice, brings you back to where you started.

73
00:04:49,766 --> 00:04:52,455
It splits a set into pairs,

74
00:04:52,455 --> 00:04:58,153
and the elements left alone are the fixed points, where f of s equals s.

75
00:04:58,233 --> 00:05:01,139
This gives a simple but powerful fact:

76
00:05:01,139 --> 00:05:04,819
the size of the set and the number of fixed points always

77
00:05:04,819 --> 00:05:06,379
have the same parity.

78
00:05:07,267 --> 00:05:10,416
Now the whole strategy is clear.

79
00:05:10,416 --> 00:05:14,694
Find a finite set S with two involutions on it.

80
00:05:14,844 --> 00:05:18,952
If the first one has exactly one fixed point,

81
00:05:18,952 --> 00:05:21,778
then S has an odd number of elements.

82
00:05:21,928 --> 00:05:28,297
So the second involution must also have an odd number of fixed points.

83
00:05:28,297 --> 00:05:30,466
In particular, at least one.

84
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:34,402
If the fixed points of the second involution are

85
00:05:34,402 --> 00:05:38,101
exactly the sums of two squares we want, we're done.

86
00:05:38,251 --> 00:05:43,174
So the questions are: what is S, and what are the two involutions?

87
00:05:46,667 --> 00:05:54,708
We want p = x² + (2y)². That is, x² + 4·y·y.

88
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:58,691
Here's the key move: relax the condition.

89
00:05:58,841 --> 00:06:02,173
Replace y times y by y times z,

90
00:06:02,173 --> 00:06:05,453
and consider all triples of positive integers (x, y,

91
00:06:05,453 --> 00:06:11,871
z) with x² + 4yz = p. Call this set S.

92
00:06:11,967 --> 00:06:18,238
Since x, y and z are positive and bounded by p, S is finite.

93
00:06:18,388 --> 00:06:22,738
For p equal to thirteen, S has just three elements:

94
00:06:22,738 --> 00:06:27,349
(1, 1, 3), (1, 3, 1), and (3, 1, 1).

95
00:06:27,433 --> 00:06:32,630
The first involution is almost a gift: just swap y and z.

96
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:36,748
Its fixed points are the triples with y equal to z,

97
00:06:36,748 --> 00:06:41,415
and then p = x² + (2y)².

98
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:45,545
That's the beauty of relaxing:

99
00:06:45,545 --> 00:06:50,726
the thing we want has become the fixed point of a symmetry.

100
00:06:50,876 --> 00:06:55,222
For thirteen, the fixed point is (3, 1, 1),

101
00:06:55,222 --> 00:06:59,479
which gives thirteen equals three squared plus two squared.

102
00:06:59,567 --> 00:07:03,758
Now we need a second involution with exactly one fixed point.

103
00:07:03,908 --> 00:07:06,878
Here is Zagier's formula, in three cases.

104
00:07:06,967 --> 00:07:10,439
At first glance, it looks like it was pulled out of thin air.

105
00:07:10,589 --> 00:07:12,327
But actually, it's a picture.

106
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:22,893
Think of x² + 4yz as an area: a square of side x in the middle,

107
00:07:22,893 --> 00:07:26,933
surrounded by four y-by-z rectangles, like a windmill.

108
00:07:27,083 --> 00:07:31,227
The total area of the windmill is exactly p.

109
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:37,274
For example, take p equal to forty-one and the triple (3, 2, 4).

110
00:07:37,424 --> 00:07:38,886
Nine cells in the middle,

111
00:07:38,886 --> 00:07:42,310
four blades of eight cells each: forty-one in total.

112
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:46,583
Every element of S is a windmill of area p.

113
00:07:47,166 --> 00:07:53,173
What does swapping y and z mean? It flips every blade over its diagonal.

114
00:07:53,323 --> 00:07:58,425
The windmill stays the same only when the blades are squares.

115
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:03,304
And four square blades fit together into one big square.

116
00:08:03,454 --> 00:08:08,617
For forty-one, that's (5, 2, 2):

117
00:08:08,617 --> 00:08:13,799
twenty-five plus sixteen, five squared plus four squared.

118
00:08:14,466 --> 00:08:18,415
Now for Zagier's map. Go back to the windmill (3, 2,

119
00:08:18,415 --> 00:08:22,290
4), erase the inner cuts, and keep only the outline.

120
00:08:22,366 --> 00:08:25,194
The outline has four inward corners.

121
00:08:25,344 --> 00:08:30,428
At each one, two edges meet. Extend one of them into the shape,

122
00:08:30,428 --> 00:08:33,050
and you recover the original windmill.

123
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:35,010
Extend the other one instead,

124
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:38,375
and you cut out a new square with four new blades.

125
00:08:38,466 --> 00:08:43,529
The new windmill is (1, 2, 5): the same outline,

126
00:08:43,529 --> 00:08:46,168
the same area, just cut differently.

127
00:08:46,318 --> 00:08:50,845
It happens to spin the other way, but that doesn't change the triple.

128
00:08:50,933 --> 00:08:55,941
So every windmill outline can be cut in exactly two ways,

129
00:08:55,941 --> 00:08:59,553
and Zagier's map simply switches between them.

130
00:08:59,703 --> 00:09:04,350
Switch twice, and you are back where you started. That's an involution.

131
00:09:05,033 --> 00:09:07,872
When are the two cuts the same?

132
00:09:07,872 --> 00:09:12,593
When the inward corners are exactly the corners of the square.

133
00:09:12,743 --> 00:09:14,955
Then the windmill is a cross:

134
00:09:14,955 --> 00:09:19,400
a unit square with four arms of length (p − 1)/4.

135
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:21,829
That's the unique fixed point.

136
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:26,934
The three cases in the formula are just bookkeeping.

137
00:09:27,084 --> 00:09:29,833
Depending on how the square compares with the blades,

138
00:09:29,833 --> 00:09:33,673
the new square gets bigger, gets smaller, or, in the middle case,

139
00:09:33,673 --> 00:09:35,786
becomes 2y − x.

140
00:09:39,266 --> 00:09:44,328
The picture gives us intuition. Now let's turn it into a rigorous proof.

141
00:09:44,478 --> 00:09:46,487
There are only five things to check.

142
00:09:46,566 --> 00:09:50,629
One: S is finite, and it's not empty.

143
00:09:50,779 --> 00:09:59,001
Since p equals 4k + 1, the triple (1, 1, k) is always in S.

144
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:03,304
Two: the three cases cover everything.

145
00:10:03,454 --> 00:10:06,522
The only possible trouble is on the boundaries:

146
00:10:06,522 --> 00:10:11,523
x exactly equal to y − z, or x exactly equal to 2y.

147
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:19,637
If x equals y − z, substituting gives p = (y + z)².

148
00:10:19,787 --> 00:10:27,061
If x equals 2y, we get p = 4y(y + z).

149
00:10:27,211 --> 00:10:32,006
A prime is neither a perfect square nor a multiple of four,

150
00:10:32,006 --> 00:10:35,071
so the boundary cases never happen.

151
00:10:35,221 --> 00:10:38,699
That's the first place we use that p is prime.

152
00:10:39,266 --> 00:10:43,619
Three: the map really sends S into S.

153
00:10:43,769 --> 00:10:49,977
Expanding the squares, x² + 4yz stays the same in every case,

154
00:10:49,977 --> 00:10:54,244
and the inequalities guarantee that the new coordinates are still

155
00:10:54,244 --> 00:10:55,151
positive integers.

156
00:10:55,733 --> 00:10:59,544
Four: it's an involution.

157
00:10:59,694 --> 00:11:02,481
Case one and case three swap with each other,

158
00:11:02,481 --> 00:11:05,681
and case two maps to itself. Apply it twice,

159
00:11:05,681 --> 00:11:07,662
and you're back to the original triple.

160
00:11:08,233 --> 00:11:13,312
Five, the crucial one: fixed points.

161
00:11:13,462 --> 00:11:15,523
Only case two can be fixed,

162
00:11:15,523 --> 00:11:23,291
and that forces x to equal y. Then p = x(x + 4z).

163
00:11:23,441 --> 00:11:30,767
Since p is prime, x must be one, and z is (p − 1)/4.

164
00:11:30,917 --> 00:11:33,105
That's the second use of primality,

165
00:11:33,105 --> 00:11:37,105
and the only place where 'one mod four' is needed:

166
00:11:37,105 --> 00:11:41,441
it guarantees that this unique fixed point actually exists.

167
00:11:41,533 --> 00:11:47,315
So Zagier's map has exactly one fixed point, the size of S is odd,

168
00:11:47,315 --> 00:11:53,221
the swap has at least one fixed point, and p is a sum of two squares.

169
00:11:53,371 --> 00:11:55,098
Q.E.D.

170
00:11:55,166 --> 00:11:57,046
Compress all of this into one sentence,

171
00:11:57,046 --> 00:12:00,338
and you get the entire text of Zagier's paper.

172
00:12:04,066 --> 00:12:08,037
Looking back, there are three ideas in this proof worth taking with you.

173
00:12:08,187 --> 00:12:10,933
They also answer the interviewer's favorite question:

174
00:12:10,933 --> 00:12:12,854
how would anyone think of this?

175
00:12:12,933 --> 00:12:16,659
First: count instead of construct.

176
00:12:16,809 --> 00:12:21,639
Searching for a and b directly gives us nothing to hold on to.

177
00:12:21,639 --> 00:12:26,919
But a statement like 'this number is odd' can often be proved by pairing

178
00:12:26,919 --> 00:12:27,781
things up.

179
00:12:27,931 --> 00:12:30,779
It's the same spirit as the handshake lemma,

180
00:12:30,779 --> 00:12:33,559
or the parity argument in Sperner's lemma.

181
00:12:33,633 --> 00:12:38,895
Second: turn what you want into the fixed points of a symmetry.

182
00:12:39,045 --> 00:12:46,213
We wanted y equal to z, so we relaxed y times y to y times z,

183
00:12:46,213 --> 00:12:52,810
and used the symmetry 'swap y and z' to pick out exactly our target.

184
00:12:52,900 --> 00:12:57,362
Third: let geometry discover the formula for you.

185
00:12:57,512 --> 00:13:02,600
As a formula, Zagier's map looks like it came from outer space.

186
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:06,571
In the windmill picture, it's just the other way to cut the same outline.

187
00:13:07,266 --> 00:13:09,126
And there's a beautiful bonus.

188
00:13:09,276 --> 00:13:13,084
If we apply the two involutions alternately,

189
00:13:13,084 --> 00:13:16,044
starting from the unique cross-shaped windmill,

190
00:13:16,044 --> 00:13:21,788
we walk along a path, and that path must end at a fixed point of the swap.

191
00:13:21,866 --> 00:13:25,461
Take forty-one. S has eleven elements.

192
00:13:25,610 --> 00:13:30,279
Starting from (1, 1, 10) and alternating swaps and windmill moves,

193
00:13:30,279 --> 00:13:33,814
we walk the whole path and stop at (5, 2, 2).

194
00:13:33,964 --> 00:13:38,648
That's forty-one equals five squared plus four squared.

195
00:13:38,798 --> 00:13:42,342
An existence proof has turned into an algorithm.

196
00:13:45,833 --> 00:13:49,346
Part of what makes a theorem great is where it leads.

197
00:13:49,496 --> 00:13:54,293
The first natural question: what about composite numbers?

198
00:13:55,066 --> 00:13:58,775
The key is an identity: a product of two sums

199
00:13:58,775 --> 00:14:02,847
of two squares is again a sum of two squares.

200
00:14:02,997 --> 00:14:06,791
It's really just multiplication of complex numbers:

201
00:14:06,791 --> 00:14:10,591
the squared length of z times the squared length of w equals the squared

202
00:14:10,591 --> 00:14:12,323
length of z w.

203
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:17,254
Combined with Fermat's theorem, this gives the complete answer:

204
00:14:17,254 --> 00:14:22,134
a positive integer is a sum of two squares if and only if every

205
00:14:22,134 --> 00:14:26,870
prime factor that is three mod four appears to an even power.

206
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:31,442
For example, 2026 is two times 1013,

207
00:14:31,442 --> 00:14:39,893
a prime that's one mod four. So 2026 = 45² + 1².

208
00:14:40,043 --> 00:14:48,136
But 2027 is itself a prime that is three mod four, so it can't be done.

209
00:14:49,333 --> 00:14:52,419
The second direction is the Gaussian integers:

210
00:14:52,419 --> 00:14:56,585
complex numbers a + bi, with a and b integers.

211
00:14:56,735 --> 00:15:01,817
In this world, p = a² + b² means exactly

212
00:15:01,817 --> 00:15:06,544
that p factors as (a + bi)(a − bi).

213
00:15:06,694 --> 00:15:13,181
Primes that are one mod four split, like 13 = (3 + 2i)(3 − 2i).

214
00:15:13,181 --> 00:15:18,571
Primes that are three mod four stay prime. And two is a special case.

215
00:15:18,666 --> 00:15:22,371
The Gaussian integers also have unique factorization,

216
00:15:22,371 --> 00:15:26,371
and that's exactly why the representation as a sum of two squares

217
00:15:26,371 --> 00:15:27,831
is unique.

218
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,837
The third direction is other quadratic forms.

219
00:15:32,987 --> 00:15:37,754
Apart from two, the primes of the form x² + 2y² are exactly

220
00:15:37,754 --> 00:15:41,994
those that are one or three mod eight. Apart from three itself,

221
00:15:41,994 --> 00:15:46,400
x² + 3y² gives the primes that are one mod three.

222
00:15:46,550 --> 00:15:50,621
Going further, the question of x² + ny² leads all the

223
00:15:50,621 --> 00:15:54,101
way to quadratic reciprocity and class field theory.

224
00:15:54,251 --> 00:15:57,018
And there's Lagrange's four-square theorem:

225
00:15:57,018 --> 00:16:00,568
every positive integer is a sum of four squares.

226
00:16:01,766 --> 00:16:06,276
Now applications. First, Pythagorean triples:

227
00:16:06,276 --> 00:16:10,356
a prime can be the hypotenuse of a right triangle with integer sides if

228
00:16:10,356 --> 00:16:12,800
and only if it's one mod four.

229
00:16:12,950 --> 00:16:17,711
For example, thirteen equals three squared plus two squared,

230
00:16:17,711 --> 00:16:21,523
which gives the triangle five, twelve, thirteen.

231
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:24,368
The most surprising one is pi.

232
00:16:24,518 --> 00:16:27,923
If you count lattice points inside a circle,

233
00:16:27,923 --> 00:16:30,723
using the formula for the number of ways to write n

234
00:16:30,723 --> 00:16:41,546
as a sum of two squares, you get π/4 = 1 − 1/3 + 1/5 − 1/7 + ….

235
00:16:41,696 --> 00:16:46,335
Remainders of primes mod four turn out to be connected to pi.

236
00:16:49,833 --> 00:16:54,046
Now let's put it all together in a mock interview.

237
00:16:54,196 --> 00:16:57,343
The questions come in four layers:

238
00:16:57,343 --> 00:17:01,953
statement, proof, conditions, and beyond.

239
00:17:02,103 --> 00:17:05,490
Listen to how each answer gives a conclusion first,

240
00:17:05,490 --> 00:17:08,995
then a reason, and then leaves a hook for the next question.

241
00:17:09,466 --> 00:17:11,780
Q: So, what's your favorite theorem?

242
00:17:11,866 --> 00:17:14,749
A: I'd say Fermat's two-square theorem.

243
00:17:14,899 --> 00:17:17,726
A: It says that an odd prime is a sum of two

244
00:17:17,726 --> 00:17:20,680
A: squares exactly when it's one mod four.

245
00:17:20,830 --> 00:17:23,230
A: What I love is the contrast:

246
00:17:23,230 --> 00:17:27,230
A: the statement is something you could explain to a middle-school student,

247
00:17:27,230 --> 00:17:32,421
A: but the proofs touch number theory, geometry, and even combinatorics.

248
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:34,429
Q: Can you state it precisely?

249
00:17:34,429 --> 00:17:37,410
Q: What happens with two, or with composite numbers?

250
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:40,109
A: Sure. For an odd prime p,

251
00:17:40,109 --> 00:17:46,630
A: p = a² + b² for some integers a and b if and only if p

252
00:17:46,630 --> 00:17:49,177
A: is congruent to one mod four.

253
00:17:49,327 --> 00:17:52,338
A: Two is also fine, since it's one plus one.

254
00:17:52,488 --> 00:17:56,095
A: For composite numbers, the rule is:

255
00:17:56,095 --> 00:18:02,791
A: every prime factor that's three mod four has to appear to an even power.

256
00:18:02,791 --> 00:18:06,382
A: So forty-five works, but twenty-one doesn't.

257
00:18:06,466 --> 00:18:08,843
Q: Why is 'one mod four' necessary?

258
00:18:08,933 --> 00:18:12,512
A: Because squares are only zero or one mod four.

259
00:18:12,662 --> 00:18:18,880
A: So a sum of two squares is zero, one, or two mod four, never three.

260
00:18:19,030 --> 00:18:24,354
A: That direction takes one line. The interesting part is the converse.

261
00:18:24,433 --> 00:18:28,498
Q: Okay. Can you sketch a proof of the converse, in about a minute?

262
00:18:28,566 --> 00:18:33,021
A: I'll use Zagier's proof. Consider the set S of

263
00:18:33,021 --> 00:18:40,996
A: positive integer triples with x² + 4yz = p. It's finite.

264
00:18:41,146 --> 00:18:45,922
A: On S there's an obvious involution: swap y and z.

265
00:18:45,922 --> 00:18:51,363
A: Its fixed points give p = x² + (2y)².

266
00:18:51,513 --> 00:18:56,346
A: Zagier found a second involution with exactly one fixed point,

267
00:18:56,346 --> 00:19:01,358
A: namely (1, 1, (p − 1)/4).

268
00:19:01,508 --> 00:19:04,298
A: Since an involution pairs up elements,

269
00:19:04,298 --> 00:19:09,428
A: the size of S has the same parity as its number of fixed points.

270
00:19:09,428 --> 00:19:14,456
A: So S has odd size, and the swap must have a fixed point too.

271
00:19:14,533 --> 00:19:17,121
Q: Where exactly did you use that p is prime?

272
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:18,658
A: In two places.

273
00:19:18,808 --> 00:19:24,949
A: First, to make Zagier's map well defined: in the boundary cases,

274
00:19:24,949 --> 00:19:28,927
A: p would be a perfect square or a multiple of four.

275
00:19:29,077 --> 00:19:32,364
A: Second, to show the fixed point is unique:

276
00:19:32,364 --> 00:19:40,905
A: a fixed point means p = x(x + 4z), and primality forces x to be one.

277
00:19:41,055 --> 00:19:46,573
A: For example, with twenty-one there are two fixed points,

278
00:19:46,573 --> 00:19:53,067
A: coming from 21 = 1 × 21 = 3 × 7.

279
00:19:53,217 --> 00:19:57,580
A: So the parity argument breaks down, and indeed,

280
00:19:57,580 --> 00:20:00,829
A: twenty-one is not a sum of two squares.

281
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:04,978
Q: That map looks like a trick. How would anyone come up with it?

282
00:20:05,066 --> 00:20:09,292
A: That was my first reaction too. The answer is a picture.

283
00:20:09,442 --> 00:20:17,383
A: x² + 4yz is the area of a windmill: a square with four rectangular blades.

284
00:20:17,533 --> 00:20:21,638
A: The outline of a windmill can be cut into a square and four blades

285
00:20:21,638 --> 00:20:26,519
A: in exactly two ways, and Zagier's map just switches between them.

286
00:20:26,669 --> 00:20:31,501
A: Once you see the picture, the formula feels inevitable.

287
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:33,598
Q: Your proof shows existence.

288
00:20:33,598 --> 00:20:37,155
Q: How would you actually find a and b for a large prime?

289
00:20:37,233 --> 00:20:42,128
A: There are two answers. The proof itself gives an algorithm:

290
00:20:42,128 --> 00:20:46,737
A: alternate the two involutions, starting from the cross-shaped windmill,

291
00:20:46,737 --> 00:20:50,960
A: and you'll walk to a solution. But that can take a long time.

292
00:20:51,110 --> 00:20:55,638
A: A much faster method is to find a square root of minus one mod p,

293
00:20:55,638 --> 00:20:59,263
A: and run the Euclidean algorithm on p and that number.

294
00:20:59,413 --> 00:21:04,082
A: The first remainder below the square root of p gives a,

295
00:21:04,082 --> 00:21:05,938
A: and then b follows.

296
00:21:06,088 --> 00:21:11,111
A: For thirteen: five squared is minus one mod thirteen,

297
00:21:11,111 --> 00:21:16,105
A: the first small remainder is three, and thirteen is nine plus four.

298
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:18,201
Q: Is the representation unique?

299
00:21:18,300 --> 00:21:20,974
A: Yes, up to order and signs.

300
00:21:21,124 --> 00:21:23,527
A: The cleanest reason is that the Gaussian

301
00:21:23,527 --> 00:21:26,050
A: integers have unique factorization.

302
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:32,140
A: Writing a² + b² = (a + bi)(a − bi),

303
00:21:32,140 --> 00:21:36,749
A: a prime that's one mod four splits into exactly two

304
00:21:36,749 --> 00:21:41,303
A: conjugate Gaussian primes, so there's essentially only one way.

305
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:43,528
Q: How would you generalize the theorem?

306
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,755
A: There are several directions.

307
00:21:45,905 --> 00:21:47,908
A: One is composite numbers,

308
00:21:47,908 --> 00:21:51,908
A: using the identity for products of sums of two squares,

309
00:21:51,908 --> 00:21:55,646
A: which is really multiplication of complex numbers.

310
00:21:55,796 --> 00:22:00,567
A: Another is other forms, like x² + 2y²,

311
00:22:00,567 --> 00:22:04,045
A: which works for primes that are one or three mod eight.

312
00:22:04,045 --> 00:22:09,105
A: And Lagrange proved that every positive integer is a sum of four squares.

313
00:22:09,255 --> 00:22:13,683
A: The deepest direction is x² + ny² in general,

314
00:22:13,683 --> 00:22:17,683
A: which leads to class field theory. I haven't studied that yet,

315
00:22:17,683 --> 00:22:20,407
A: but it's something I'm really curious about.

316
00:22:20,500 --> 00:22:23,337
Q: Is the theorem useful for anything beyond itself?

317
00:22:23,433 --> 00:22:27,607
A: One example I like is Pythagorean triples:

318
00:22:27,607 --> 00:22:32,998
A: a prime is the hypotenuse of an integer right triangle exactly

319
00:22:32,998 --> 00:22:34,965
A: when it's one mod four.

320
00:22:35,115 --> 00:22:37,845
A: A more surprising one is pi.

321
00:22:37,845 --> 00:22:41,672
A: Counting lattice points in a circle with the two-square formula gives

322
00:22:41,672 --> 00:22:47,968
A: the Leibniz series: π/4 = 1 − 1/3 + 1/5 − ….

323
00:22:48,066 --> 00:22:52,295
Q: If you had to keep only one idea from this proof, what would it be?

324
00:22:52,366 --> 00:22:56,428
A: Probably the parity trick: to prove that something exists,

325
00:22:56,428 --> 00:22:59,485
A: show that the number of such things is odd.

326
00:22:59,635 --> 00:23:02,297
A: It's surprisingly general.

327
00:23:02,297 --> 00:23:06,880
A: The same idea appears in the handshake lemma and in Sperner's lemma.

328
00:23:07,030 --> 00:23:10,919
A: And what I find beautiful is that a counting argument,

329
00:23:10,919 --> 00:23:13,789
A: which seems totally non-constructive,

330
00:23:13,789 --> 00:23:17,819
A: can be turned into an algorithm by following the pairs.

331
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:21,988
Q: Quick one: is 2027 a sum of two squares?

332
00:23:22,066 --> 00:23:29,954
A: No. 2027 is a prime, and it's three mod four, so it's impossible.

333
00:23:30,104 --> 00:23:35,972
A: But 2026 is: it's forty-five squared plus one squared.

334
00:23:36,066 --> 00:23:41,737
Notice the pattern: conclusion first, then the reason, then a hook.

335
00:23:41,887 --> 00:23:45,753
That's how you lead the conversation toward what you know best.

336
00:23:49,233 --> 00:23:51,461
Let me end with a personal thought.

337
00:23:51,611 --> 00:23:55,659
What moves me about this theorem is not just the result,

338
00:23:55,659 --> 00:24:00,046
but how many different eyes have seen the same fact.

339
00:24:00,196 --> 00:24:08,011
Fermat and Euler saw descent. Gauss saw factorization of complex numbers.

340
00:24:08,011 --> 00:24:13,111
Minkowski saw lattice points. Zagier saw pairing.

341
00:24:13,800 --> 00:24:17,756
A good proof doesn't just verify a result.

342
00:24:17,756 --> 00:24:21,372
It tells you why the result could not have been otherwise.

343
00:24:21,522 --> 00:24:24,192
Zagier's sentence made me believe the theorem.

344
00:24:24,192 --> 00:24:26,708
The windmill made me understand it.

345
00:24:27,400 --> 00:24:30,348
So if an interviewer asks for your favorite theorem,

346
00:24:30,348 --> 00:24:32,669
the best answer isn't a memorized proof.

347
00:24:32,819 --> 00:24:38,049
It's what you saw, where you got stuck, and what you still want to ask.

348
00:24:38,733 --> 00:24:43,163
Every prime that is one mod four is a sum of two squares.

349
00:24:43,313 --> 00:24:45,492
Next time you see thirteen, twenty-nine,

350
00:24:45,492 --> 00:24:48,421
or forty-one, I hope you see a windmill.
